   "text": "I read this as a macrocyclic peptide and wrote each residue in sequence round the ring. The one uncertain part is the wavy-bond fragment on the ornithine \u03b1-nitrogen, which I took as an isopropyl group.\n\n**Residues, going round the ring from the ornithine:**\n- **Ornithine:** its \u03b1-nitrogen carries the fragment described above. Its \u03b4-nitrogen is acylated by the carbonyl of the next residue.\n- **Homophenylalanine:** a CH2CH2Ph side chain.\n- **Cyclopropylglycine.**\n- **2-Aminobutyric acid:** an ethyl side chain.\n- **Glutamic acid:** a free CH2CH2COOH side chain.\n- **Glycine.**\n- **Valine.**\n- **Histidine:** an imidazole side chain.\n- **Cyclopropylglycine.**\n- **Proline:** its nitrogen is acylated by the ornithine carbonyl, which closes the macrocycle.\n\n**The wavy-bond fragment:** the image crosses the N\u2013C bond with a wavy line, so the fragment is truncated. I treated the carbon beside the nitrogen as a CH bearing two methyls, giving N\u2013CH(CH3)2.\n\n**Stereochemistry:** I worked out each \u03b1-carbon from its wedge and the position of its neighbours. All ten stereocentres come out as L-configured amino acids, matching the wedges drawn.\n\nSMILES: CC(C)N[C@H]1CCCNC(=O)[C@H](CCc2ccccc2)NC(=O)[C@H](C2CC2)NC(=O)[C@H](CC)NC(=O)[C@H](CCC(=O)O)NC(=O)CNC(=O)[C@H](C(C)C)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc2c[nH]cn2)NC(=O)[C@H](C2CC2)NC(=O)[C@H]2CCCN2C1=O"
